Subject: RegExp: pristine fast paths for test/exec/match/search/replace/split, direct lastIndex, a kept backtrack stack

What this does
  - quickjs.c: the context records the shapes of RegExp.prototype and RegExp and the functions of the properties the algorithms read (exec, constructor, flags, the flag getters, source, Symbol.match, Symbol.species). `js_regexp_fast` accepts a RegExp that has the original shape with an integer lastIndex and an unchanged prototype; it is checked after every step that can run user code.
  - For such a RegExp: `test` skips the exec lookup; `exec`/`test` read and write lastIndex in the slot; `[Symbol.search]` runs the matcher once and leaves lastIndex alone; global `[Symbol.match]` and `[Symbol.replace]` (string or function replacement, $-patterns, no named groups, not sticky-only) loop on the matcher over capture offsets with no result objects; `[Symbol.split]` with a RegExp species skips the splitter object (and its recompile) and scans with the existing prefilter loop.
  - libregexp.c: the backtrack stack that outgrows the in-frame buffer is kept per thread between matches; growth is first checked against the context's allocator limit.

Why we need it
  Census on a small repro: a sticky splitter was compiled per split call; replace/match built a result array and string per match. Interpreter timings on a repro: replace 4x, match 3x, split 2x, search 2x.

Soundness
  Any change to the object's shape, to the prototype's shape or to one of the recorded functions sends the call to the general code. Tests: .local/kit/regexp-fast.js compares each operation on a pristine RegExp with the same one given an extra own property, across flags, lastIndex states and prototype changes.

Failure modes
  A regexp created through `new RegExp` or a subclass may carry another shape and take the general path. The kept stack is at most 1M entries per thread.

